{"title":"2002 IMO Shortlist Problems/N3","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solutions\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solutions</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Resources\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Resources</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/N3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/5/ec5ef07965a3e362e603f70698fc4efbf81a7fb1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_{1}, p_{2}, \\ldots , p_{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"99\" height=\"11\" /> be distinct primes greater than 3.  Show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/a/24a47d1cc2aeb7004b34cb4cd41019e9f3c0b9ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{p_{1}p_{2} \\cdots p_{n}} + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"93\" height=\"14\" /> has at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/7/6c78734258d2d82aeb28c1631e8f1798154c888d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"13\" /> divisors.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solutions\">Solutions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/N3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solutions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/N3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><b>Lemma 1</b>.  If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/f/37f09c4bea354fc3b8f9d0ce2f877f9e64a7dca9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k &gt; m$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"50\" height=\"13\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/a/c3a10718e17d1487c92957fec8bfbf91b23b94ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$km$\" width=\"25\" height=\"12\" /> has at least twice as many divisors as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p><i>Proof</i>.  For every divisor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/8/408730e185e0b41fcc93fd334b48e79f1c8e799d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ka$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> is clearly a divisor of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/a/c3a10718e17d1487c92957fec8bfbf91b23b94ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$km$\" width=\"25\" height=\"12\" />, but not <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p><b>Lemma 2</b>.  If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/d/9ad99798ec4c38e165cf517cb9e02b1c9e824103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/f/a9f23bf124b6b2b2a993eb313c72e678664ac74a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> are <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Relatively_prime\" title=\"Relatively prime\">relatively prime</a> odd numbers, then the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Greatest_common_factor\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Greatest common factor\">greatest common factor</a> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/5/b15f7c293115530504b9eeb73d99769a8226fb5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^u + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"48\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/b/ffb9630370a2163e5ec5a20f2274a8196ba4a3aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^v + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"47\" height=\"14\" /> is 3.\n</p><p><i>Proof</i>.  Clearly, 3 divides both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/5/b15f7c293115530504b9eeb73d99769a8226fb5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^u + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"48\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/b/ffb9630370a2163e5ec5a20f2274a8196ba4a3aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^v + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"47\" height=\"14\" />.  Now, suppose that there is some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/a/68a4efae9c4d2ec151e703617c3f2cf32210cd8b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t &gt; 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"39\" height=\"13\" /> which divides both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/5/b15f7c293115530504b9eeb73d99769a8226fb5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^u + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"48\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/b/ffb9630370a2163e5ec5a20f2274a8196ba4a3aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^v + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"47\" height=\"14\" />.  Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/6/086716f7b9c3566bce3e740c17e475e2835f8cbb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^u \\equiv 2^v \\equiv -1 \\pmod{t}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"174\" height=\"18\" />.  But this means that both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/d/9ad99798ec4c38e165cf517cb9e02b1c9e824103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/f/a9f23bf124b6b2b2a993eb313c72e678664ac74a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> are divisible by half the order of 2 in mod <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/d/e0d2bf360290fd61d1c1557e763f2622363b3d35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$t$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"13\" />, contradicting our assumption that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/d/9ad99798ec4c38e165cf517cb9e02b1c9e824103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/f/a9f23bf124b6b2b2a993eb313c72e678664ac74a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> are relatively prime.\n</p><p>We now note that since\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/d/64d9cee44b647981b2e3e91bac2b029d8f693a90.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{uv} = (2^{u} + 1) \\left[ \\sum_{i=0}^{v-1} (-2)^{ui} \\right] \\qquad \\qquad$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"281\" height=\"55\" />,\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>we know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/8/cf8bed1c701fe570dfb41bf70b7a322ecd9987df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{uv} + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"55\" height=\"14\" /> is divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/5/b15f7c293115530504b9eeb73d99769a8226fb5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^u + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"48\" height=\"14\" />, and, by symmetry, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/2/ac24085cac62d8e3847e4ce17f5479a273e67bb8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^v$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" /> also, and therefore also by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/1/0a18be026fce8b0086aec403f4f2a26318a323d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2^u + 1)(2^v +1)/3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"143\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>We now prove the result by induction on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.  Our base case, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/9/1d96ecc15129c5631ba4a2c6578e91e3d13e3baf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, is easy, since we know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/a/32aa2f4629b452b01adc1a23e4a1c7cdf17ece7f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{p_1} + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"53\" height=\"14\" /> is divisible by 3, and is greater than 27.  Now, set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/9/e9997fd92353681b41f340b1171d8eb94d607e5c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u = p_{1}\\cdots p_{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"111\" height=\"11\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/8/7782b6b053103b8eba1a1773e24e6a86985f31f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v = p_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"50\" height=\"11\" />.  By Lemma 2, we know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/c/cdc66a5964b1415030308445f1f674c83a1015f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{u} + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"48\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/e/18e87033aea1fe782a2b15048f6d4dc44f57a3d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{v} + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"47\" height=\"14\" /> are relatively prime; hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/7/0c79809ffff663a1a049c464435def5aec72698d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2^u +1)(2^v + 1)/3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"143\" height=\"18\" /> has at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/f/dcf7f9f56ea2018cc50c9a7f44fadf2dfbd32710.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\cdot 4^{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"55\" height=\"15\" /> factors, by the inductive hypothesis.\n</p><p>We know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/7/0c79809ffff663a1a049c464435def5aec72698d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2^u +1)(2^v + 1)/3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"143\" height=\"18\" /> divides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/8/cf8bed1c701fe570dfb41bf70b7a322ecd9987df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{uv} + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"55\" height=\"14\" />.  We also know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/7/d271a899b7a7ed70cce7f86ffadb1a9528b2e4fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$uv &gt; 2(u+v)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"111\" height=\"20\" />, whence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/b/2ab88a211877b71aa106aeeb7e261726d1f3e8e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{uv} &gt; \\left[ (2^u + 1)(2^v + 1)/3 \\right]^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"212\" height=\"23\" />.  Then by Lemma 1,\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/7/077fd4697d904e3315c4c2ee9afa29e4f18ea06c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d(2^{uv} + 1) \\ge d \\left[ (2^{u}+1)(2^{v}+1)/3 \\right] \\ge 4^{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"311\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>Q.E.D.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/N3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>We proceed by induction.  For our base case, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/6/c766f9fce6070981e62f805a7cf7294baed4498e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, we note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/a/60a364467501707b541ed92032ca1e98bc3de219.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{p_1} +1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"53\" height=\"14\" /> is divisible by 3.  Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/b/9abaed9ebeacb384761411fb2de9df66ecef6d9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_1 \\ge 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"50\" height=\"16\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/a/60a364467501707b541ed92032ca1e98bc3de219.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{p_1} +1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"53\" height=\"14\" /> is clearly greater than 3 and cannot be a larger power of three, since this would require <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/e/38efe708a2e17ccdef63bc237ec34a2d95055e67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_1 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"121\" height=\"18\" />.  Therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/a/32aa2f4629b452b01adc1a23e4a1c7cdf17ece7f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{p_1} + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"53\" height=\"14\" /> has some other prime factor and therefore at least 4 factors overall.\n</p><p>Now, suppose the proposition holds for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/c/93cf7e6f6bb911e44d1eadce1799608f8730bab2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = k-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"75\" height=\"13\" />.  Without loss of generality, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/e/b5e24a94b7aabff2c1ecf896e7e55e6633137aaf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_{k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"17\" height=\"11\" /> be the minimum of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/e/4ee4c99001cb9b86307d1c4f931c8f03d845cbb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_{1}, \\ldots , p_{k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"74\" height=\"11\" />.  We shall abbreviate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/f/f4f09748f90868543f84f5b5815aff2c8e01b5b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q = \\prod_{i=1}^{k-1}p_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"74\" height=\"51\" />.  We note the relation\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/2/d422d5d521b63914a1575265683743ab1fb4148f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{qp_k} + 1 = \\left( 2^q + 1 \\right) \\left[ \\sum_{i=0}^{p_{k}-1} (-2)^{qi} \\right]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"247\" height=\"53\" />.\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/2/2e246391642225f5c9f2c540fc39f81b3e8ced59.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^q + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> has at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/8/3d89eebb31770ba02f06f1d5fc2bf20965d06d7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4^{k-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"32\" height=\"16\" /> factors, it will suffice to show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/9/ea98f62f55c6dbb032fd57d19d6c2e93ffa26c33.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{i=0}^{p_{k}-1} (-2)^{qi}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"80\" height=\"51\" /> is relatively prime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/2/2e246391642225f5c9f2c540fc39f81b3e8ced59.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^q + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, and has at least four factors.\n</p><p>We note that in general, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/b/c9baa5cf9e3c6ec38a8b857fd76b047718cc8b7f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{i=0}^{p_{k}-1}(-x)^{i}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"74\" height=\"51\" /> has remainder <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/0/4508393135451dc6c41b01f36072dd16674586bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"17\" height=\"11\" /> when divided by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/c/cccb103143393c0119f5ab61d6d53a852ce70fcf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" />.  But <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/0/4508393135451dc6c41b01f36072dd16674586bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"17\" height=\"11\" /> cannot divide <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/2/2e246391642225f5c9f2c540fc39f81b3e8ced59.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^q + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, as this would require <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/c/83c033843c6696112aa56c3f19d420fa6529f73c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^q \\equiv -1 \\pmod{p_k-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"174\" height=\"18\" /> when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is relatively prime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/e/3de9acc44f1e1f68cb000ce52262dbee9e402552.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_k - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"48\" height=\"15\" />.  Hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/9/ea98f62f55c6dbb032fd57d19d6c2e93ffa26c33.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{i=0}^{p_{k}-1} (-2)^{qi}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"80\" height=\"51\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/2/2e246391642225f5c9f2c540fc39f81b3e8ced59.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^q + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> are relatively prime.\n</p><p>We now claim that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/2/002832024d0572cc62931a094971d73419a7ad0f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{i=0}^{p_{k}-1}(-2)^{qi}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"80\" height=\"51\" /> has at least four factors.  We start by noting that in general, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/9/a0963ef6c838fc3d006678ce8f5fe5396869be5a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{i=0}^{p_{k}-1} x^i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"52\" height=\"56\" /> divides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/2/7d233e2b8ec93c5b4305c04f6e45c987132fe470.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{i=0}^{p_{k}-1} x^{qi}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"56\" height=\"51\" />, since the roots of the former are the nonreal <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/e/b5e24a94b7aabff2c1ecf896e7e55e6633137aaf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_{k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"17\" height=\"11\" />th <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Roots_of_unity\" title=\"Roots of unity\">roots of unity</a> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/1/e811d16235cc96f6a23ba3644938e848b4e908d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q \\perp p_{k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"49\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p><p>Since clearly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/6/8a6b8536ce2ad8178f3fb9947fda6290571be97b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{i=0}^{p_{k}-1} (-2)^{qi} &gt; \\sum_{i=0}^{p_{k}-1} (-2)^i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"178\" height=\"51\" />, it is sufficient to show that the former is not the square of the latter (i.e., that the former is either a higher power of the latter, or some other prime divides the latter, either of which implies that the former has at least four factors).  But this follows from\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/e/e6e9a3bd379f0968c1fd40d26f92bbac33bfe490.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{matrix} \\sum_{i=0}^{p_{k}-1} (-2)^{qi} &amp; &gt; &amp; 2^{q(p_{k}-1) - 1} \\quad \\\\ &amp; &gt; &amp; \\left( 2^{p_{k}} \\right)^2 \\qquad \\quad \\\\ &amp; &gt; &amp;\\left[ \\sum_{i=0}^{p_{k}-1} (-2)^i \\right]^2 \\end{matrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -36px\" width=\"264\" height=\"81\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>Thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/9/ea98f62f55c6dbb032fd57d19d6c2e93ffa26c33.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{i=0}^{p_{k}-1} (-2)^{qi}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"80\" height=\"51\" /> has at least four factors and is relatively prime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/9/fa9a55162859406dd36b24ca37d331198570929e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{q} + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, completing the induction.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/N3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>We proceed by induction. Base case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/9/1d96ecc15129c5631ba4a2c6578e91e3d13e3baf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />. Observe that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/3/173c628e7e7f9cf7db0188cd7ec370cd939490d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3|2^{p_1}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"18\" />, and since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/2/612aba5bc2842bdc51680bfb29031907b112ee17.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_1&gt;3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"50\" height=\"16\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/d/bfda5ec2180ba75d99afdad34d8948e4d44f5195.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{p_1}+1&gt;9 \\implies \\frac{2^{p_{1}}+1}{3}&gt;3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"228\" height=\"37\" /> so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/e/96e79ba6c8d0a34a66cdc0bb988cb0839b5ecfce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{p_1}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"53\" height=\"14\" /> has at least 4 divisors.\n</p><p>Now suppose the proposition holds for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/a/1aa3a6afd518eb941fd311e157ec2b571f7376ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=h$\" width=\"45\" height=\"12\" />. \n</p><p><br />\nlemma 1:\nif <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/e/7fe35ce2f78014de6bf5865fb76445b7e2aab7aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$gcd(a,b)=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"98\" height=\"18\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> both being odd, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/2/1a2998ef0ad1295a8205ff459e3e3c99f18c206d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$gcd(2^a+1,2^b+1)=3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"177\" height=\"20\" />. \n</p><p>Proof:\nlet p be a prime that divides both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/e/6fe111085cca11aed707f75dffe44e7077eaf8ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^a+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"47\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/8/8d8a1aceec30a59f2bdc4faa35df77de02f1e097.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^b+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"17\" />. Then p divides both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/3/2535cc204166e79b2683ec568fc742af96966d9a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{2a}-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"54\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/c/b5c202f345d400cb51638e8afdc5a1b25a0679d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{2b}-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"52\" height=\"16\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/1/22123741d302da477ef5e364f082a5dab7c6319a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{2a}\\equiv 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"56\" height=\"15\" /> (mod <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />) and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/e/7deba350c27dc7624d0213fd195853239dc8b673.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{2b}\\equiv 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"54\" height=\"16\" /> (mod <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />), <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/c/bcc045e44ac227ddccbf5cb7177ebb680b9c3002.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ord_{p}\\left(2\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" /> divides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/6/6c6ea4756efa52a156029529aba37a305a5cb496.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/b/a9b30259d95f64f9aa7c6bd36e1583618a23c8bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"19\" height=\"14\" />. But, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/e/7fe35ce2f78014de6bf5865fb76445b7e2aab7aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$gcd(a,b)=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"98\" height=\"18\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/2/732619735c9f2c7b0b305bd4f1c3250ce87681ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ord_{p}\\left(2\\right)=2 \\implies p=3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"185\" height=\"18\" />. Hence proven.\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><br />\nlemma 2:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/1/f11d07f916fb0b416ff67a02fb0515a6b0a32d91.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3||2^p+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" />, with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> being a prime greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Proof;\nIt suffices to show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/2/1b21a842887dd9ecac9cc4da749208f40d61f0c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9\\nmid 2^p+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"71\" height=\"18\" />. for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/b/81b8ce0cbe2cdfa2faa6d6f006155a063dde350a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0\\le i\\le8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"72\" height=\"15\" />, the only solution to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/4/c243760092d8ec9bbd3caab217e0da004f9cdce1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^i+1\\equiv 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"78\" height=\"16\" /> (mod <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />) is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/5/a4566e11dab22bafa7c82220f54e440ef4437ff3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i=3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"41\" height=\"14\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is the smallest natural <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/9/df9786bb2614934b53be496f86c56b4433bcf213.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^k-1\\equiv 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"81\" height=\"16\" /> (mod <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />). This means that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/d/f9d664524e352c6b466c24a01e1d0de10ffb6c5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^m+1\\equiv 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"86\" height=\"14\" /> (mod <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />), <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/f/afff1e6f7dcadb86e0e2673c386b5ed32eea7fef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m\\equiv 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" /> (mod <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />), but <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is a prime greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, so this is not possible.\n</p><p>Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/0/1f051b5d10fe11ce13f2ca911f5690ecff04048c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd\\left(2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}}+1,\\ 2^{p_{n+1}}+1\\right)=3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"256\" height=\"18\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/a/ceac2cdc1ad5de67bfe03982bbc07d6810d62c28.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=\\frac{2^{p_{n+1}}+1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"112\" height=\"37\" />. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/a/eeacafa98a3b8fac5f81be04b9774156549257a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}}+1\\right)\\left(2^{p_{n+1}}+1\\right)&lt;3\\left(2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}p_{n+1}}+1\\right) \\implies \\left(2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}}+1\\right)m&lt;\\left(2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}p_{n+1}}+1\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"708\" height=\"18\" />. So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/7/3772438819b6fdbb3a7ff2e6ff0305032d908e65.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}p_{n+1}}+1=\\left(2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}}+1\\right)mx$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"286\" height=\"18\" />. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/1/da1274575c6d1cbff418e66fb525ec4f5ece522b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$gcd(m,2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}}+1)=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"192\" height=\"18\" />. \n</p><p>I will now prove <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/5/5d5dfc3ebcc1a6a9d5920f4f589e3c4124dc43a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&gt;2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"128\" height=\"14\" />. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/6/826cfb1beb82457216876ac3aec4a2f98487fe9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\frac{3\\left(2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}p_{n+1}}+1\\right)}{\\left(2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}}+1\\right)\\left(2^{p_{n+1}}+1\\right)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"233\" height=\"43\" />. We wish to prove <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/a/b7ac6bf53eece0b9fcc8163dc69f5af05624edfb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\frac{3\\left(2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}p_{n+1}}+1\\right)}{\\left(2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}}+1\\right)\\left(2^{p_{n+1}}+1\\right)}&gt;2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"353\" height=\"43\" />. Rearrange the inequality to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/b/93b72b3e046a49b36f365f8335c14534468fee04.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(2+1\\right)\\left(2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}p_{n+1}}+1\\right)&gt;\\left(2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}}+1\\right)^{2}\\left(2^{p_{n+1}}+1\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"422\" height=\"21\" />. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/9/0b98dc897152f22042d24204be62896053cc7a04.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}p_{n+1}+1&gt;2p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}+p_{n+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"321\" height=\"16\" />, which implies the inequality. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/5/5d5dfc3ebcc1a6a9d5920f4f589e3c4124dc43a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&gt;2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"128\" height=\"14\" />,  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/0/8805f3a3fab0a91b1761d9ba3dc101293803bbca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\nmid 2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"119\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>Also, note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/0/da0e83434a62388258f87ac650ca6a92dea96f21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}}+1&gt;m \\implies x&gt;m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"234\" height=\"14\" />. This means <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/f/d4f98dc2eebbe3ccf9bc13622bdadb41fd85e87d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$mx$\" width=\"26\" height=\"8\" /> has at least four factors. Note that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/b/0dbbfc8e8e0c13cdff8b35a7a2a5d383ace123d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$gcd(x,2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}}+1)=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"186\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/f/d4f98dc2eebbe3ccf9bc13622bdadb41fd85e87d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$mx$\" width=\"26\" height=\"8\" /> would be relatively prime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/6/8c6cf6c5839aee4b3f0711af3fa27d37ea336e79.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"93\" height=\"14\" />, which would mean <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/f/76f39ae2a1a3aa9c20deaacc9a53b0d308c7d3f8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}p_{n+1}}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"123\" height=\"14\" /> has at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/8/12898933329214e4040370d51d3ac489b3dd5ac8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4\\cdot4^{h}=4^{h+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"96\" height=\"16\" /> factors.\n</p><p>Assume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> are not relatively prime. Then there exists some prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/f/e4faf1254596bfbb9060ca5675b1bee2b7b8b8ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_{q}\\left(x\\right)&gt;v_{q}\\left(2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}}+1\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"194\" height=\"18\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/2/49275e4f16783c6a03122a7110d1a892c20e4163.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}p_{n+1}}+1=q_{1}^{e_{1}}q_{2}^{e_{2}}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot q_{k}^{e_{k}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"245\" height=\"17\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/b/1bb61a7b1602fd5a2c03b6e9b6f11a17e12c6253.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=pD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"64\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/e/9ae71f36cd0198b6bf877ffced71f16a9d44f1a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=kq_{1}^{e_{1}+f}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"83\" height=\"20\" />, with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/3/ed356153c46770cbdcf78e77989540a408802bcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"13\" height=\"11\" /> being the prime in question. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/e/cee9aa9a1daf7aabb21a785d4abe83c8104cec43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f\\ge1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" />. (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is relatively prime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/6/8c6cf6c5839aee4b3f0711af3fa27d37ea336e79.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}}+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"93\" height=\"14\" />). Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/7/477c1c8cec8ceb447b7f7421c6242fbbbe780a31.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}p_{n+1}}+1=q_{1}^{2e_{1}+f}q_{2}^{e_{2}}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot q_{k}^{e_{k}}p\\cdot D\\cdot k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"332\" height=\"20\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/5/b05725a6508fe8df761c6e91d2735b697ebd87d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" /> be the number of divisors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/4/aa4bfbc4b491a8cd1c23322e08ace10a93cb09b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d\\left(q_{1}^{2e_{1}+f}q_{2}^{e_{2}}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot q_{k}^{e_{k}}p\\cdot D\\cdot k\\right)\\ge d\\left(q_{1}^{2e_{1}+f}q_{2}^{e_{2}}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot q_{k}^{e_{k}}p\\right)=\\left(2e_{1}+f+1\\right)\\left(e_{2}+1\\right)\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot\\left(e_{k}+1\\right)\\cdot2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"719\" height=\"34\" />. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/4/b346a3a50b9d2798418c69840ab61128a0f5ac4c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f+1\\ge 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"75\" height=\"16\" />, so \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/9/69943b1758cd611e932a7220c9332918488f0c19.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2e_{1}+f+1\\ge2\\left(e_{1}+1\\right) \\implies \\left(2e_{1}+f+1\\right)\\left(e_{2}+1\\right)\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot\\left(e_{k}+1\\right)\\cdot2\\ge4\\left(e_{1}+1\\right)\\left(e_{2}+1\\right)\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot\\left(e_{k}+1\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"774\" height=\"18\" />. From the inductive step <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/4/324f61ec5fe1fa8dad750662c4a254782e4d7b6c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d(2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}}+1)=\\left(e_{1}+1\\right)\\left(e_{2}+1\\right)\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot\\left(e_{k}+1\\right)\\ge 4^h$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"402\" height=\"20\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/3/9331ef59fc421eee38997ae7ec139e187d8180d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4\\left(e_{1}+1\\right)\\left(e_{2}+1\\right)\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot\\left(e_{k}+1\\right)\\ge 4^{h+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"288\" height=\"20\" />. This implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/1/2f13c5884c679c0edd63e01afe397db0999d4370.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d(2^{p_{1}p_{2}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot p_{n}p_{n+1}}+1)\\ge 4^{h+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"204\" height=\"20\" />, which completes the induction. \nQED\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Resources\">Resources</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/N3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Resources\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_IMO_Shortlist_Problems\" title=\"2002 IMO Shortlist Problems\">2002 IMO Shortlist Problems</a></li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/viewtopic.php?t=17326\">Discussion on AoPS/MathLinks</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018022511\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.049 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.062 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 815/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 31082/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-